::GEO_U05_L03_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L03_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>In cyclic quadrilateral 1, one angle measures 70&deg;. What is the measure of its opposite angle? {
=A. 110&deg;#Opposite angles of an inscribed quadrilateral are supplementary, so 180&deg; - 70&deg; = 110&deg;.
~B. 70&deg;#This treats opposite angles as congruent, but cyclic quadrilateral opposite angles are supplementary.
~C. 90&deg;#This adds without using the 180&deg; supplementary relationship.
~D. Cannot be determined#The opposite angle can be determined because cyclic quadrilateral opposite angles are supplementary.
}

::GEO_U05_L03_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L03_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>Triangle 2 is inscribed in a circle with one side as a diameter. If one acute angle is 28&deg;, what is the other acute angle? {
~A. 28&deg;#The acute angles are not necessarily congruent.
=B. 62&deg;#An angle that intercepts a diameter is 90&deg;, so the two acute angles sum to 90&deg;.
~C. 118&deg;#This ignores the triangle angle sum and the right angle from the diameter.
~D. 90&deg;#The angle opposite the diameter is 90&deg;, not the remaining acute angle.
}

::GEO_U05_L03_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L03_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>An inscribed triangle in diagram 3 has an angle intercepting an arc of 100&deg;. What is that angle measure? {
~A. 100&deg;#This would match a central angle, not an inscribed angle.
~B. 200&deg;#This doubles the arc instead of halving it.
=C. 50&deg;#An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc is given, so the inscribed angle can be determined.
}

::GEO_U05_L03_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L03_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>Quadrilateral 4 is inscribed in a circle. Which pair of opposite angle measures could be valid? {
~A. 64&deg; and 64&deg;#Equal opposite angles are not required; they must be supplementary.
~B. 64&deg; and 84&deg;#These values do not show the required 180&deg; sum for opposite angles.
~C. 90&deg; and 45&deg;#These angles sum to 135&deg;, not 180&deg;.
=D. 64&deg; and 116&deg;#Opposite angles in an inscribed quadrilateral must have a sum of 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U05_L03_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L03_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.6<br><br>Question:<br>In circle diagram 5, two inscribed angles intercept the same arc. What can be concluded? {
=A. The two inscribed angles are congruent.#Inscribed angles intercepting the same arc have equal measures.
~B. The two angles are supplementary.#Same intercepted arc supports congruence, not necessarily supplementary angles.
~C. Each angle equals the full arc measure.#An inscribed angle is half its intercepted arc.
~D. No conclusion is possible.#The shared intercepted arc gives enough evidence for a conclusion.
}

